# Django security

Narrows `python`'s security guidance to Django's own injection-class sinks:
views, forms/serializers, template-tag modules, and settings — the Python
files where Django's built-in CSRF/XSS/SQL-injection protections are either
relied on or bypassed (template-rendered `.html` files are not `.py`, so
they are out of this rule's `paths:` scope by construction, even though the
XSS guidance below still applies to what those views/templatetags render
into them). Applies only to the listed `.py` files; a non-request-facing
module (a standalone script, a batch job with no HTTP surface) still
follows `python`'s own `rules/security.mdc`.

## CSRF

- Leave `django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware` enabled and let
  `{% csrf_token %}`/the framework's CSRF cookie handling do its job for
  every state-changing view (`POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH`/`DELETE`).
- Never add `@csrf_exempt` to make a failing request succeed without first
  establishing that the endpoint has another, equivalent protection (a
  signed webhook signature, a non-browser API authenticated by token, never
  session/cookie auth) — CSRF exemption on a session-authenticated endpoint
  reopens the exact attack the middleware exists to block.
- A DRF `APIView`/`ViewSet` using `SessionAuthentication` still needs CSRF
  protection on unsafe methods (DRF enforces this by default for session
  auth); do not disable it to work around a client that isn't sending the
  token — fix the client to send it.

## Cross-site scripting (XSS)

- Rely on Django's automatic template auto-escaping for any value that
  reaches an HTML template — do not disable it project-wide.
- Never apply `mark_safe()`/the `|safe` template filter/`format_html()`'s
  raw-string argument to a value that traces back to user input (a form
  field, a query parameter, an uploaded file's name, anything stored from a
  prior user submission) — `mark_safe`/`|safe` tell Django to skip escaping
  entirely, and reintroduce stored or reflected XSS on exactly the value the
  auto-escaper was protecting. `mark_safe`/`|safe` are for markup the
  application itself constructs and fully controls (a trusted CMS field
  rendered through a sanitizer, static admin-authored HTML) — never for
  data supplied by a request.
- Build dynamically-assembled HTML with `format_html()`/`format_html_join()`
  (which escape each interpolated argument) instead of string
  concatenation or an f-string passed to `mark_safe()`.

## SQL injection

- Django's ORM parameterizes query values automatically
  (`.filter(name=user_input)`); this protection does not extend to
  `.raw()` or `.extra()` — pass user-derived values through the `params`
  argument (`Model.objects.raw("... WHERE id = %s", [id])`), never by
  interpolating them into the SQL string with an f-string/`%`/`.format()`.
- Treat `.extra()` as legacy; prefer expressing the same query with the ORM
  (`annotate`, `F()`, `Q()`, `Func`) or a parameterized `.raw()` call — the
  Django security team explicitly does not treat unsanitized input reaching
  `.extra()`'s `select`/`where` clauses as a framework bug, because
  `.extra()` was never given the ORM's own escaping.

## Settings and secrets

- `SECRET_KEY` must be at least 50 characters, unique per environment, never
  the `django-insecure-` placeholder `startproject` generates, and never
  committed to source — read it from the environment/secret store.
- `DEBUG` must be `False` in any settings module used in production;
  `DEBUG = True` leaks stack traces, local variable values, and settings
  contents to any visitor who triggers an unhandled exception.
- `ALLOWED_HOSTS` must be a real, non-empty list of the application's actual
  hostnames in production, never `["*"]` — an empty or wildcard value
  defeats the Host-header validation Django's deploy checks exist to
  enforce.
- Run `python manage.py check --deploy` before a production release; it
  flags exactly these three (and related HSTS/secure-cookie) settings
  automatically — don't hand-audit `settings.py` for them instead.

## Red flags

- "I'll add `|safe` here, this content only ever comes from an admin form" —
  if that admin form field can ever hold end-user-influenced text (a
  support agent pasting a customer's message, a bio field editable by the
  account owner), the trust boundary already includes untrusted input.
- "`.extra()` with an f-string is fine, this value is just an internal ID"
  — an "internal" ID that ever originates from a URL param, form field, or
  header is user-controlled regardless of how it's described; use `params`.
- "`@csrf_exempt` unblocks this POST and the tests pass now" — passing
  tests after exempting CSRF proves the exemption works, not that it's
  safe; verify the endpoint has an equivalent protection first.
